00:08:34.440 --> 00:08:40.280
So in the first part, I just want to give a bit&nbsp;
of an overview of a topic that I've worked on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.280 --> 00:08:46.200
but that, I think is more broadly, could be&nbsp;
broadly useful to scientists who also do a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.200 --> 00:08:53.040
lot of scientific modeling, which is some&nbsp;
results, some correspondences that we built&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.040 --> 00:08:59.480
between neural networks and Gaussian processes,&nbsp;
which I won't describe in a lot of detail,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.480 --> 00:09:05.440
but maybe some of you are familiar with, it's&nbsp;
sort of a very different modeling paradigm

00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:07.080
to

00:09:08.400 --> 00:09:15.960
to the training of neural networks.&nbsp;
So just to set up the basic problem,

00:09:17.000 --> 00:09:22.280
let me consider a just a vanilla neural&nbsp;
network. But we can do this for a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.280 --> 00:09:27.520
different architectures, a fully connected&nbsp;
deep neural network, which has a certain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.520 --> 00:09:34.360
depth L to the computation that it does,&nbsp;
and it has a certain hidden layer with n,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.360 --> 00:09:41.960
which is essentially how many computations&nbsp;
it's Doing in parallel at the same time. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.960 --> 00:09:46.680
so we can describe, you know what the&nbsp;
computation done by this network is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.680 --> 00:09:54.120
recursion relationship where the function f is a&nbsp;
function of the original input data x coming in,

00:09:55.840 --> 00:09:59.960
and we take the signal FL minus&nbsp;
one, which is also a function.

00:09:59.960 --> 00:10:07.480
Of x, let's say, at coming in here, we pass it&nbsp;
through a non linearity phi. We multiply by weight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.480 --> 00:10:14.200
matrices, which are our learnable parameters in&nbsp;
this network, and maybe a bias. And we do that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.200 --> 00:10:21.800
to construct this signal at the next layer. And&nbsp;
this continues through the through the network.

00:10:21.800 --> 00:10:28.520
So this computation, as I mentioned, it has&nbsp;
a depth to it, and it has a network width,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.520 --> 00:10:33.160
where, here you sum over all these contributions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.160 --> 00:10:38.440
from the different hidden nodes or&nbsp;
neurons from the previous layer.

00:10:39.040 --> 00:10:43.600
Now, in general, being saying, being able&nbsp;
to say something about this computation is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.600 --> 00:10:48.080
pretty challenging. It depends on a&nbsp;
lot of the hyper parameters. But it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.080 --> 00:10:53.160
turns out that at large n in this so&nbsp;
when you take the limit of a large&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.160 --> 00:11:01.280
number of hidden nodes or neurons, the&nbsp;
description of the system simplifies.

00:11:02.080 --> 00:11:06.560
So you can kind of see that through&nbsp;
the central limit theorem and or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.600 --> 00:11:12.840
other more rigorous techniques. But&nbsp;
essentially what happens is, at large n

00:11:14.480 --> 00:11:17.600
you sum over very many

00:11:18.560 --> 00:11:25.800
variables here, and that sum you can when, when&nbsp;
you start with at least at the at the beginning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.800 --> 00:11:31.880
of training, a random network will involve the&nbsp;
sum over many random variables. And you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.880 --> 00:11:38.000
imagine how you apply central limit theorem&nbsp;
to that to be able to describe the process&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.000 --> 00:11:44.720
F. So this kind of ties in. Basically, it's&nbsp;
studying the statistical physics of large and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.720 --> 00:11:50.600
deep neural networks at the moment, just when&nbsp;
they're randomly initialized. So we're just,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.600 --> 00:11:55.520
we're you can think of it being at the&nbsp;
beginning of training, we initialize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.520 --> 00:12:01.200
the parameters randomly, and then we want to be&nbsp;
able to describe the system. But I'll talk about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.200 --> 00:12:08.920
training in just a minute, because it turns out&nbsp;
we you can just easily then get to that as well.

00:12:08.920 --> 00:12:14.080
So this is also, you can think of it as a type&nbsp;
of mean field theory that becomes exact in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.080 --> 00:12:20.400
infinite width limit. So the conclusion is&nbsp;
that at the large in the large end limit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.400 --> 00:12:25.960
when the network width is wide enough, this is&nbsp;
well described by a Gaussian process. This neural&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.960 --> 00:12:35.400
network, and what we need to describe a Gaussian&nbsp;
process is just a mean and a covariance function.

00:12:35.960 --> 00:12:41.960
So it's just described by its low order&nbsp;
statistics. So it turns out that that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.960 --> 00:12:47.120
also pretty natural, easy to calculate. So&nbsp;
there's some recursion that happens on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:47.120 --> 00:12:51.920
space of functions as you go through&nbsp;
one layer of the network to another,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.920 --> 00:12:55.560
which I just described. It's&nbsp;
this Iterative computation,

00:12:56.200 --> 00:13:03.840
but if you were going to calculate the covariance&nbsp;
function, so the expectation of f of x times f of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.840 --> 00:13:13.400
x prime averaged over the network, this is what&nbsp;
you need to be able to describe the to construct&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.400 --> 00:13:19.360
the kernel function of the Gaussian process.&nbsp;
It turns out that that also obeys a recursion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:19.360 --> 00:13:27.200
relation. So for from F naught, you can compute&nbsp;
a k naught, from f1 you can compute a k1 and so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.200 --> 00:13:35.440
on until you get to a final K. This covariance&nbsp;
function, or kernel function, is a function of

00:13:35.440 --> 00:13:40.600
two inputs, x and x prime. So just as a

00:13:40.600 --> 00:13:46.960
maybe reminder so a Gaussian process I mentioned&nbsp;
needs a mean function and a covariance function.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:46.960 --> 00:13:51.920
The mean function in this setting is just&nbsp;
zero. The covariance function is a function&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.920 --> 00:13:59.520
of two arguments, x and x prime. It tells&nbsp;
you roughly how similar X and X prime are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:59.520 --> 00:14:05.480
after you perform this series of computations,&nbsp;
and there's a recursion relationship that you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.480 --> 00:14:11.920
get for K that exactly mirrors the one we wrote&nbsp;
down for F, because it's done iteratively, it

00:14:13.960 --> 00:14:21.480
inherits the computation that the&nbsp;
neural Network inherently is doing.

00:14:21.480 --> 00:14:29.040
So this is a way, a natural way, in which Gaussian&nbsp;
processes and kernels arise from neural networks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:29.800 --> 00:14:34.720
naturally, and they have a certain structure&nbsp;
that's different from kernels or Gaussian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:34.720 --> 00:14:41.720
processes that were known before, because they&nbsp;
have this compositionality in them. And you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:41.720 --> 00:14:47.320
classic yacht processes in the literature&nbsp;
and textbooks, if you look at them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:47.320 --> 00:14:52.080
have a very different structure. They're&nbsp;
usually constructed from simpler functions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:52.080 --> 00:15:00.040
and they're not compositional in&nbsp;
this way that I described. So.

00:15:04.240 --> 00:15:10.000
So now that was the study of random networks,&nbsp;
but once you map the problem over to a Gaussian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:10.000 --> 00:15:15.120
process, and that description is exact, as&nbsp;
I mentioned in that limit, and it's a good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:15.120 --> 00:15:24.320
description, if the layer width is large enough,&nbsp;
then it's easy to do Bayesian learning from that.

00:15:24.880 --> 00:15:31.320
So if you have a data set D, and you'd&nbsp;
like to make Bayesian predictions from this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:31.320 --> 00:15:38.200
neural network in this large width limit, you can&nbsp;
essentially do that through this Gaussian process,

00:15:38.720 --> 00:15:45.040
for example. So these are now well&nbsp;
written, you know, kind of textbook,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:45.040 --> 00:15:53.480
Gaussian processes. There are equations that you&nbsp;
can look up for what the prediction would be after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:53.480 --> 00:16:01.160
learning on a certain amount of data, for the for&nbsp;
the network or for the model. So, for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:01.160 --> 00:16:07.720
the output of the function on some new point&nbsp;
x is it's a probabilistic computation. So it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:07.720 --> 00:16:13.760
described by some distribution. In this case, it's&nbsp;
a normal distribution with a mean and a variance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:13.760 --> 00:16:19.360
that you can write down analytically. And for&nbsp;
example, the mean is just gotten by computing this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:19.360 --> 00:16:27.360
linear algebra, you know, computation on the tart,&nbsp;
on the original targets, y, at the data points&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:27.360 --> 00:16:34.240
that you have, and doing some linear algebra using&nbsp;
this kernel function that I described on the prior&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:34.240 --> 00:16:41.760
slide. So for every network, every neural network&nbsp;
that you have, you have this kernel function k you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:41.760 --> 00:16:46.880
can compute it on your training data, just&nbsp;
as you would for other Gaussian processes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:46.880 --> 00:16:54.920
and then you can use that to make predictions.&nbsp;
And so this became a way of obtaining Bayesian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:54.920 --> 00:17:02.200
predictions that are exact from an infinitely&nbsp;
wide, deep neural network. And pictorially for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:02.200 --> 00:17:10.000
those of you, just as a maybe reminder of Bayesian&nbsp;
kind of methods and what a Gaussian process does.

00:17:10.000 --> 00:17:14.000
You know it's probabilistic,&nbsp;
so before you learn from data,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:14.000 --> 00:17:19.680
you have some kind of distribution&nbsp;
over the values of the function

00:17:21.200 --> 00:17:27.720
with some mean and some variance. But after you&nbsp;
learn from data, your function kind of reduces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:27.720 --> 00:17:34.680
its error around those data points to match&nbsp;
the values y that it's regressing to. And then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:34.680 --> 00:17:41.440
there's some remaining variants in the places in&nbsp;
input space where you don't have a lot of data,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:41.440 --> 00:17:47.680
but you would need to, you know, have&nbsp;
more constraints, to do better learning.

00:17:47.680 --> 00:17:53.480
And so in summary, this was kind of&nbsp;
a few year program, essentially to

00:17:53.480 --> 00:17:58.840
take neural networks of different architectures,&nbsp;
so this zoo of architectures that exists out there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:59.840 --> 00:18:06.280
with all sorts of different models&nbsp;
and derive, in this large end limit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:06.280 --> 00:18:12.320
the Gaussian process and kernels that&nbsp;
originate from those. And we did that at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:12.320 --> 00:18:17.160
the very beginning with our very first paper&nbsp;
on just the fully connected neural network,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:17.160 --> 00:18:24.347
which gives rise to some kernel k, which I&nbsp;
label FC, just to just to say that it it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:24.880 --> 00:18:30.280
tied to the architecture of a fully connected&nbsp;
neural network. You can do something similar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:30.280 --> 00:18:36.120
with a convolutional neural network. So&nbsp;
these are often used in vision problems.

00:18:37.200 --> 00:18:43.000
There the notion of what is n, what is the large&nbsp;
width limit is slightly different. It's the number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:43.000 --> 00:18:49.320
of filters that you use. But again, it's always&nbsp;
corresponding to this sequential process, sorry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:49.320 --> 00:18:53.520
parallel processing that you're doing&nbsp;
in every layer at the same time. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:53.520 --> 00:18:58.800
